We would increase our headcount, to be clear. But we would increase our output. The units produced per human... The total number of humans at Tesla will increase, but the output of robots

420
01:30:09,120 --> 01:30:23,040
and cars will increase disproportionately. The number of cars and robots produced per human will increase dramatically, but the number of humans will increase as well.

421
01:30:23,040 --> 01:30:39,440
We're talking about Chinese manufacturing a bunch here. We've also talked about some of the policies that are relevant, like you mentioned, the solar tariffs. You think they're a bad idea because

422
01:30:39,440 --> 01:30:50,480
we can't scale up solar in the US. Electricity output in the US needs to scale up. It can't without good power sources. You just need to get it somehow.

423
01:30:50,480 --> 01:31:01,600
Where I was going with this is, if you were in charge, if you were setting all the policies, what else would you change? You’d change the solar tariffs, that’s one.

424
01:31:01,600 --> 01:31:10,560
I would say anything that is a limiting factor for electricity needs to be addressed, provided it's not very bad for the environment. So presumably some permitting reforms and stuff

425
01:31:10,560 --> 01:31:21,760
as well would be in there? There's a fair bit of permitting reforms that are happening. A lot of the permitting is state-based, but anything federal... This administration is good at

426
01:31:21,760 --> 01:31:32,000
removing permitting roadblocks. I'm not saying all tariffs are bad. Solar tariffs. Sometimes if another country is

427
01:31:32,000 --> 01:31:43,600
subsidizing the output of something, then you have to have countervailing tariffs to protect domestic industry against subsidies by another country. What else would you change?

428
01:31:43,600 --> 01:31:57,520
I don't know if there's that much that the government can actually do. One thing I was wondering... For the policy goal of creating a lead for the US versus China,

429
01:31:57,520 --> 01:32:07,680
it seems like the export bans have actually been quite impactful, where China is not producing leading-edge chips and the export bans really bite there.

430
01:32:07,680 --> 01:32:16,000
China is not producing leading-edge turbine engines. Similarly, there's a bunch of export bans that are relevant there on some of the metallurgy.

431
01:32:16,000 --> 01:32:24,880
Should there be more export bans? As you think about things like the drone industry and things like that, is that something that should be considered?

432
01:32:24,880 --> 01:32:40,520
It's important to appreciate that in most areas, China is very advanced in manufacturing. There's only a few areas where it is not. China is a manufacturing powerhouse, next-level.

433
01:32:40,520 --> 01:33:00,320
It's very impressive. If you take refining of ore, China does roughly twice as much ore refining on average as the rest of the world combined.

434
01:33:00,320 --> 01:33:10,480
There are some areas, like refining gallium which goes into solar cells. I think they are 98% of gallium refining. So China is actually very advanced

435
01:33:10,480 --> 01:33:20,480
in manufacturing in most areas. It seems like there is discomfort with this supply chain dependence, and yet nothing's really happening on it.

436
01:33:20,480 --> 01:33:31,760
Supply chain dependence? Say, like the gallium refining that you're saying. All the rare-earth stuff. Rare earths for sure,

437
01:33:31,760 --> 01:33:45,120
as you know, they’re not rare. We actually do rare earth ore mining in the US, send the rock, put it on a train, and then put it on a boat to China that goes to another train,

438
01:33:45,120 --> 01:33:54,720
and goes to the rare earth refiners in China who then refine it, put it into a magnet, put it into a motor sub-assembly, and then send it back to America.

439
01:33:54,720 --> 01:34:12,560
So the thing we're really missing is a lot of ore refining in America. Isn't this worth a policy intervention? Yes. I think there are some things being done on that front. But we kind of need Optimus,

440
01:34:12,560 --> 01:34:24,480
frankly, to build ore refineries. So, you think the main advantage China has is the abundance of skilled labor? That's the thing Optimus fixes?

441
01:34:24,480 --> 01:34:34,880
Yes. China’s got like four times our population. I mean, there's this concern. If you think human resources are the future, right now if it's the skilled labor for manufacturing

442
01:34:34,880 --> 01:34:44,400
that's determining who can build more humanoids, China has more of those. It manufactures more humanoids, therefore it gets the Optimi future first.

443
01:34:44,400 --> 01:34:52,640
Well, we’ll see. Maybe. It just keeps that exponential going. It seems like you're sort of pointing out that getting to a million Optimi requires

444
01:34:52,640 --> 01:35:01,600
the manufacturing that the Optimi is supposed to help us get to. Right? You can close that recursive loop pretty quickly. With a small number of Optimi?

445
01:35:01,600 --> 01:35:13,200
Yeah. So you close the recursive loop to help the robots build the robots. Then we can try to get to tens of millions of units a year. Maybe. If you start getting

446
01:35:13,200 --> 01:35:23,040
to hundreds of millions of units a year, you're going to be the most competitive country by far. We definitely can't win with just humans, because China has four times our population.

447
01:35:23,040 --> 01:35:31,840
Frankly, America has been winning for so long that… A pro sports team that's been winning for a very long time tends to get complacent and entitled.

448
01:35:31,840 --> 01:35:44,000
That's why they stop winning, because they don't work as hard anymore. So frankly my observation is just that the average work ethic in China is higher than in the US.

449
01:35:44,000 --> 01:35:52,320
It's not just that there's four times the population, but the amount of work that people put in is higher. So you can try to rearrange the humans,

450
01:35:52,320 --> 01:36:06,960
but you're still one quarter of the—assuming that productivity is the same, which I think actually it might not be, I think China might have an advantage on productivity per person—we will do

451
01:36:06,960 --> 01:36:20,400
one quarter of the amount of things as China. So we can't win on the human front. Our birth rate has been low for a long time. The US birth rate's been below replacement

452
01:36:20,400 --> 01:36:38,640
since roughly 1971. We've got a lot of people retiring, we're close to more people domestically dying than being born. So we definitely can't win on the human front,

453
01:36:38,640 --> 01:36:48,400
but we might have a shot at the robot front. Are there other things that you have wanted to manufacture in the past, but they've been too labor intensive or too expensive that now you

454
01:36:48,400 --> 01:37:00,080
can come back to and say, "oh, we can finally do the whatever, because we have Optimus?" Yeah, we'd like to build more ore refineries at Tesla.

455
01:37:00,080 --> 01:37:12,560
We just completed construction and have begun lithium refining with our lithium refinery in Corpus Christi, Texas. We have a nickel refinery,

456
01:37:12,560 --> 01:37:35,840
which is for the cathode, that's here in Austin. This is the largest cathode refinery, largest nickel and lithium refinery, outside of China. The cathode team would say, "we have the

457
01:37:35,840 --> 01:37:43,200
largest and the only, actually, cathode refinery in America." Not just the largest, but it's also the only. Many superlatives.

458
01:37:43,200 --> 01:38:04,000
So it was pretty big, even though it's the only one. But there are other things. You could do a lot more refineries and help America be more competitive on refining capacity.

459
01:38:04,000 --> 01:38:15,200
There's basically a lot of work for the Optimus to do that most Americans, very few Americans, frankly want to do. Is the refining work too dirty or what's the—

460
01:38:15,200 --> 01:38:29,280
It's not actually, no. We don't have toxic emissions from the refinery or anything. The cathode nickel refinery is in Travis County. Why can't you do it with humans?

461
01:38:29,280 --> 01:38:36,160
You can, you just run out of humans. Ah, I see. Okay. No matter what you do, you have one quarter of the number of humans in America than China.

462
01:38:36,160 --> 01:38:48,960
So if you have them do this thing, they can't do the other thing. So then how do you build this refining capacity? Well, you could do it with Optimi.

463
01:38:48,960 --> 01:39:04,480
Not very many Americans are pining to do refining. I mean, how many have you run into? Very few. Very few pining to refine. BYD is reaching Tesla

464
01:39:04,480 --> 01:39:19,280
production or sales in quantity. What do you think happens in global markets as Chinese production in EVs scales up? China is extremely competitive in manufacturing.

465
01:39:19,280 --> 01:39:37,040
So I think there's going to be a massive flood of Chinese vehicles and basically most manufactured things. As it is, as I said, China is probably

466
01:39:37,040 --> 01:39:50,320
doing twice as much refining as the rest of the world combined. So if you go down to fourth and fifth-tier supply chain stuff…

467
01:39:50,320 --> 01:40:03,840
At the base level, you've got energy, then you've got mining and refining. Those foundation layers are, like I said, as a rough guess, China's doing twice as much refining

468
01:40:03,840 --> 01:40:14,640
as the rest of the world combined. So any given thing is going to have Chinese content because China's doing twice as much refining work as the rest of the world.

469
01:40:14,640 --> 01:40:26,240
But they'll go all the way to the finished product with the cars. I mean China is a powerhouse. I think this year China will exceed

470
01:40:26,240 --> 01:40:39,680
three times US electricity output. Electricity output is a reasonable proxy for the economy. In order to run the factories

471
01:40:39,680 --> 01:40:55,280
and run everything, you need electricity. It's a good proxy for the real economy. If China passes three times the US electricity output,

472
01:40:55,280 --> 01:41:06,320
it means that its industrial capacity—as rough approximation—will be three times that of the US. Reading between the lines, it sounds like what you're saying is absent some sort of humanoid

473
01:41:06,320 --> 01:41:23,120
recursive miracle in the next few years, on the whole manufacturing/energy/raw materials chain, China will just dominate whether it comes to AI or manufacturing EVs or manufacturing humanoids.

474
01:41:23,120 --> 01:41:49,520
In the absence of breakthrough innovations in the US, China will utterly dominate. Interesting. Yes. Robotics being the main breakthrough innovation. Well, to scale AI in space, basically you need

475
01:41:49,520 --> 01:42:03,920
humanoid robots, you need real-world AI, you need a million tons a year to orbit. Let's just say if we get the mass driver on the moon going, my favorite thing, then I think—

476
01:42:03,920 --> 01:42:18,640
We'll have solved all our problems. I call that winning. I call it winning, big time. You can finally be satisfied. You've done something. Yes. You have the mass driver on the moon.

477
01:42:18,640 --> 01:42:26,480
I just want to see that thing in operation. Was that out of some sci-fi or where did you…? Well, actually, there is a Heinlein book. The Moon is a Harsh Mistress.

478
01:42:26,480 --> 01:42:35,040
Okay, yeah, but that's slightly different. That's a gravity slingshot or... No, they have a mass driver on the Moon. Okay, yeah, but they use that to attack Earth.

479
01:42:35,040 --> 01:42:40,720
So maybe it's not the greatest... Well they use that to… assert their independence. Exactly. What are your plans for the mass driver on the Moon?

480
01:42:40,720 --> 01:42:48,080
They asserted their independence. Earth government disagreed and they lobbed things until Earth government agreed. That book is a hoot. I found that

481
01:42:48,080 --> 01:42:58,120
book much better than his other one that everyone reads, Stranger in a Strange Land. "Grok" comes from Stranger in a Strange Land. The first two-thirds of Stranger in a Strange

482
01:42:58,120 --> 01:44:18,400
Land are good, and then it gets very weird in the third portion. But there are still some good concepts in there. One thing we were discussing a lot

483
01:44:18,400 --> 01:44:32,560
is your system for managing people. You interviewed the first few thousand of SpaceX employees and lots of other companies. It obviously doesn't scale. Well, yes, but what doesn't scale? Me.

484
01:44:32,560 --> 01:44:42,800
Sure, sure. I know that. But what are you looking for? There literally are not enough hours in the day. It's impossible. But what are you looking for that someone else who's good at interviewing

485
01:44:42,800 --> 01:44:56,400
and hiring people… What's the je ne sais quoi? At this point, I might have more training data on evaluating technical talent especially—talent of all kinds I suppose, but technical talent

486
01:44:56,400 --> 01:45:11,680
especially—given that I've done so many technical interviews and then seen the results. So my training set is enormous and has a very wide range.

487
01:45:11,680 --> 01:45:27,680
Generally, the things I ask for are bullet points for evidence of exceptional ability. These things can be pretty off the wall. It doesn't need to be in the specific domain,

488
01:45:27,680 --> 01:45:39,600
but evidence of exceptional ability. So if somebody can cite even one thing, but let's say three things, where you go, "Wow, wow, wow," then that's a good sign.

489
01:45:39,600 --> 01:45:48,720
Why do you have to be the one to determine that? No, I don't. I can't be. It's impossible. The total headcount across all companies is 200,000 people.

490
01:45:48,720 --> 01:46:02,320
But in the early days, what was it that you were looking for that couldn't be delegated in those interviews? I guess I need to build my training set.

491
01:46:02,320 --> 01:46:10,240
It's not like I batted a thousand here. I would make mistakes, but then I'd be able to see where I thought somebody would work out well, but they didn't.

492
01:46:10,240 --> 01:46:22,560
Then why did they not work out well? What can I do, I guess RL myself, to in the future have a better batting average when interviewing people?

493
01:46:22,560 --> 01:46:30,080
My batting average is still not perfect, but it's very high. What are some surprising reasons people don't work out? Surprising reasons… Like, they don't understand

494
01:46:30,080 --> 01:46:43,920
technical domain, et cetera, et cetera. But you've got the long tail now of like, "I was really excited about this person. It didn't work out." Curious why that happens.

495
01:46:43,920 --> 01:46:55,600
Generally what I tell people—I tell myself, I guess, aspirationally—is, don't look at the resume. Just believe your interaction. The resume may seem very impressive and it's like,

496
01:46:55,600 --> 01:47:07,360
"Wow, the resume looks good." But if the conversation after 20 minutes is not "wow," you should believe the conversation, not the paper.

497
01:47:07,360 --> 01:47:19,760
I feel like part of your method is that… There was this meme in the media a few years back about Tesla being a revolving door of executive talent. Whereas actually, I think when you look at it,

498
01:47:19,760 --> 01:47:29,600
Tesla's had a very consistent and internally promoted executive bench over the past few years. Then at SpaceX, you have all these folks like Mark Juncosa and Steve Davis—

499
01:47:29,600 --> 01:47:43,280
Steve Davis runs The Boring Company these days. Bill Riley, and folks like that. It feels like part of what has worked well is having very capable technical deputies.

500
01:47:43,280 --> 01:48:03,280
What do all of those people have in common? Well, the Tesla senior team, at this point has probably got an average tenure of 10-12 years. It's quite long.

501
01:48:03,280 --> 01:48:17,360
But there were times when Tesla went through an extremely rapid growth phase, so things were just somewhat sped up. As you know, a company goes through

502
01:48:17,360 --> 01:48:28,160
different orders of magnitude of size. People that could help manage, say, a 50-person company versus a 500-person company versus a 5,000-person company versus

503
01:48:28,160 --> 01:48:39,600
a 50,000-person company. You outgrew people. It's just not the same team. It's not always the same team. So if a company is growing very rapidly, the rate at which executive positions will

504
01:48:39,600 --> 01:48:56,720
change will also be proportionate to the rapidity of the growth generally. Tesla had a further challenge where when Tesla had very successful periods, we would be relentlessly

505
01:48:56,720 --> 01:49:10,880
recruited from. Like, relentlessly. When Apple had their electric car program, they were carpet bombing Tesla with recruiting calls. Engineers just unplugged their phones.

506
01:49:10,880 --> 01:49:19,120
"I'm trying to get work done here." Yeah. "If I get one more call from an Apple recruiter…" But their opening offer without any interview would be

507
01:49:19,120 --> 01:49:32,160
like double the compensation at Tesla. So we had a bit of the "Tesla pixie dust" thing where it's like, "Oh, if you hire a Tesla executive,

508
01:49:32,160 --> 01:49:41,600
suddenly everything's going to be successful." I've fallen prey to the pixie dust thing as well, where it's like, "Oh, we'll hire someone from Google or Apple and they'll be immediately

509
01:49:41,600 --> 01:49:57,840
successful," but that's not how it works. People are people. There's no magical pixie dust. So when we had the pixie dust problem, we would get relentlessly recruited from.

510
01:49:57,840 --> 01:50:10,080
Also, Tesla being engineering, especially being primarily in Silicon Valley, it's easier for people to just... They don't have to change their life very much.

511
01:50:10,080 --> 01:50:21,200
Their commute's going to be the same. So how do you prevent that? How do you prevent the pixie dust effect where everyone's trying to poach all your people?

512
01:50:21,200 --> 01:50:39,920
I don't think there's much we can do to stop it. That's one of the reasons why Tesla… Really, being in Silicon Valley and having the pixie dust thing at the same time meant that there was

513
01:50:39,920 --> 01:50:56,240
just a very, very aggressive recruitment. Presumably being in Austin helps then? Austin, it helps. Tesla still has a majority of its engineering in California.

514
01:50:56,240 --> 01:51:06,160
Getting engineers to move… I call it the "significant other" problem. Yes, "significant others" have jobs. Exactly. So for Starbase that was

515
01:51:06,160 --> 01:51:22,240
particularly difficult, since the odds of finding a non-SpaceX job… In Brownsville, Texas… …are pretty low. It's quite difficult. It's like a technology monastery thing, remote and mostly dudes.

516
01:51:22,240 --> 01:51:41,680
Not much of an improvement over SF. If you go back to these people who've really been very effective in a technical capacity at Tesla, at SpaceX, and those sorts of places, what

517
01:51:41,680 --> 01:51:52,160
do you think they have in common other than... Is it just that they're very sharp on the rocketry or the technical foundations, or do you think it's something organizational?

518
01:51:52,160 --> 01:52:08,560
Is it something about their ability to work with you? Is it their ability to be flexible but not too flexible? What makes a good sparring partner for you? I don't think of it as a sparring partner.

519
01:52:08,560 --> 01:52:17,760
If somebody gets things done, I love them, and if they don't, I hate them. So it's pretty straightforward. It's not like some idiosyncratic thing.

520
01:52:17,760 --> 01:52:25,840
If somebody executes well, I'm a huge fan, and if they don't, I'm not. But it's not about mapping to my idiosyncratic preferences.

521
01:52:25,840 --> 01:52:46,960
I certainly try not to have it be mapping to my idiosyncratic preferences. Generally, I think it's a good idea to hire for talent and drive and trustworthiness.

522
01:52:46,960 --> 01:53:01,200
And I think goodness of heart is important. I underweighted that at one point. So, are they a good person? Trustworthy? Smart and talented and hard working?

523
01:53:01,200 --> 01:53:14,480
If so, you can add domain knowledge. But those fundamental traits, those fundamental properties, you cannot change. So most of the people who are at Tesla and SpaceX

524
01:53:14,480 --> 01:53:21,680
did not come from the aerospace industry or the auto industry. What has had to change most about your management style as your companies have

525
01:53:21,680 --> 01:53:39,200
scaled from 100 to 1,000 to 10,000 people? You're known for this very micro management, just getting into the details of things. Nano management, please. Pico management. Femto management. Keep going.

526
01:53:39,200 --> 01:53:52,800
We're going to go all the way down to Planck's constant. All the way down to Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Are you still able to get into details as much as you want?

527
01:53:52,800 --> 01:53:58,640
Would your companies be more successful if they were smaller? How do you think about that? Because I have a fixed amount of

528
01:53:58,640 --> 01:54:17,440
time in the day, my time is necessarily diluted as things grow and as the span of activity increases. It's impossible for me to actually be a micromanager because that would imply I

529
01:54:17,440 --> 01:54:31,040
have some thousands of hours per day. It is a logical impossibility for me to micromanage things. Now, there are times when I will drill down into a

530
01:54:31,040 --> 01:54:49,440
specific issue because that specific issue is the limiting factor on the progress of the company. The reason for drilling into some very detailed item is because it is the limiting factor.

531
01:54:49,440 --> 01:55:03,040
It’s not arbitrarily drilling into tiny things. From a time standpoint, it is physically impossible for me to arbitrarily go into tiny things that don't matter. That would

532
01:55:03,040 --> 01:55:18,560
result in failure. But sometimes the tiny things are decisive in victory. Famously, you switched the Starship design from composites to steel. Yes. You made

533
01:55:18,560 --> 01:55:25,280
that decision. That wasn't people going around saying, "Oh, we found something better, boss." That was you encouraging people against some resistance.

534
01:55:25,280 --> 01:55:45,600
Can you tell us how you came to that whole concept of the steel switch? Desperation, I'd say. Originally, we were going to make Starship out of carbon fiber.

535
01:55:45,600 --> 01:56:00,720
Carbon fiber is pretty expensive. When you do volume production, you can get any given thing to start to approach its material cost. The problem with carbon fiber is that

536
01:56:00,720 --> 01:56:20,800
material cost is still very high. Particularly if you go for a high-strength specialized carbon fiber that can handle cryogenic oxygen, it's roughly 50 times the cost of steel.

537
01:56:20,800 --> 01:56:35,520
At least in theory, it would be lighter. People generally think of steel as being heavy and carbon fiber as being light. For room temperature applications,

538
01:56:35,520 --> 01:56:48,000
like a Formula 1 car, static aero structure, or any kind of aero structure really, you're probably going to be better off with carbon fiber. The problem is that we were trying to make this

539
01:56:48,000 --> 01:56:57,040
enormous rocket out of carbon fiber and our progress was extremely slow. It had been picked in the first place just because it's light?

540
01:56:57,040 --> 01:57:18,000
Yes. At first glance, most people would think that the choice for making something light would be carbon fiber. The thing is that when you make something very

541
01:57:18,000 --> 01:57:33,280
enormous out of carbon fiber and then you try to have the carbon fiber be efficiently cured, meaning not room temperature cured, because sometimes you got 50 plies of carbon fiber…

542
01:57:33,280 --> 01:57:46,320
Carbon fiber is really carbon string and glue. In order to have high strength, you need an autoclave. Something that's essentially a high pressure oven.

543
01:57:46,320 --> 01:57:58,720
If you have something that's gigantic, that one's got to be bigger than the rocket. We were trying to make an autoclave that's bigger than any autoclave that's ever existed.

544
01:57:58,720 --> 01:58:12,480
Or you can do room temperature cure, which takes a long time and has issues. The final issue is that we were just making very slow progress with carbon fiber.

545
01:58:12,480 --> 01:58:20,880
The meta question is why it had to be you who made that decision. There's many engineers on your team. How did the team not arrive at steel?

546
01:58:20,880 --> 01:58:29,840
Yeah exactly. This is part of a broader question, understanding your comparative advantage at your companies. Because we were making very slow

547
01:58:29,840 --> 01:58:41,520
progress with carbon fiber, I was like, "Okay, we've got to try something else." For the Falcon 9, the primary airframe is made of aluminum lithium, which has

548
01:58:41,520 --> 01:58:53,440
a very good strength-to-weight. Actually, it has about the same, maybe better, strength to weight for its application than carbon fiber. But aluminum lithium is very difficult to work with.

549
01:58:53,440 --> 01:59:02,800
In order to weld it, you have to do something called friction stir welding, where you join the metal without entering the liquid phase. It's kind of wild that you can do that.

550
01:59:02,800 --> 01:59:16,320
But with this particular type of welding, you can do that. It's very difficult. Let's say you want to make a modification or attach something to aluminum lithium, you now have to use a mechanical

551
01:59:16,320 --> 01:59:34,960
attachment with seals. You can't weld it on. So I wanted to avoid using aluminum lithium for the primary structure for Starship. There was this very special grade of

552
01:59:34,960 --> 01:59:48,800
carbon fiber that had very good mass properties. With a rocket, you're really trying to maximize the percentage of the rocket that is propellant, minimize the mass obviously.

553
01:59:48,800 --> 02:00:01,360
But like I said, we were making very slow progress. I said, "at this rate, we’re never going to get to Mars. So we've got to think of something else." I didn't want to use aluminum lithium

554
02:00:01,360 --> 02:00:12,880
because of the difficulty of friction stir welding, especially doing that at scale. It was hard enough at 3.6 meters in diameter, let alone at 9 meters or above.

555
02:00:12,880 --> 02:00:30,880
Then I said, "what about steel?" I had a clue here because some of the early US rockets had used very thin steel. The Atlas rockets had used a steel balloon tank.

556
02:00:30,880 --> 02:00:46,640
It's not like steel had never been used before. It actually had been used. When you look at the material properties of stainless steel, full-hard, strain hardened stainless steel,

557
02:00:46,640 --> 02:00:58,560
at cryogenic temperature the strength to weight is actually similar to carbon fiber. If you look at material properties at room temperature, it looks like

558
02:00:58,560 --> 02:01:10,240
the steel is going to be twice as heavy. But if you look at the material properties at cryogenic temperature of full-hard steel, stainless of particular grades,

559
02:01:10,240 --> 02:01:19,760
then you actually get to a similar strength to weight as carbon fiber. In the case of Starship, both the fuel and the oxidizer are cryogenic.

560
02:01:19,760 --> 02:01:38,080
For Falcon 9, the fuel is rocket propellant-grade kerosene, basically a very pure form of jet fuel. That is roughly room temperature. Although we do actually chill it slightly below,

561
02:01:38,080 --> 02:01:50,000
we chill it like a beer. Delicious. We do chill it, but it's not cryogenic. In fact, if we made it cryogenic, it would just turn to wax. But for Starship,

562
02:01:50,000 --> 02:02:03,600
it's liquid methane and liquid oxygen. They are liquid at similar temperatures. Basically, almost the entire primary structure is at cryogenic temperature.

563
02:02:03,600 --> 02:02:17,680
So then you've got a 300-series stainless that's strain hardened. Because almost all things are cryogenic temperature, it actually has similar

564
02:02:17,680 --> 02:02:30,800
strength to weight as carbon fiber. But it costs 50x less in raw material and is very easy to work with. You can weld stainless steel outdoors.

565
02:02:30,800 --> 02:02:44,560
You could smoke a cigar while welding stainless steel. It's very resilient. You can modify it easily. If you want to attach something, you just weld it right on.

566
02:02:44,560 --> 02:03:02,720
Very easy to work with, very low cost. Like I said, at cryogenic temperature, it’s similar strength-to-weight to carbon fiber. Then when you factor in that we have a much

567
02:03:02,720 --> 02:03:13,680
reduced heat shield mass, because the melting point of steel, is much greater than the melting point of aluminum… It's about twice the melting point of aluminum.

568
02:03:13,680 --> 02:03:25,440
So you can just run the rocket much hotter? Yes, especially for the ship which is coming in like a blazing meteor. You can greatly reduce

569
02:03:25,440 --> 02:03:45,840
the mass of the heat shield. You can cut the mass of the windward part of the heat shield, maybe in half, and you don't need any heat shielding on the leeward side.

570
02:03:45,840 --> 02:04:00,560
The net result is that actually the steel rocket weighs less than the carbon fiber rocket, because the resin in the carbon fiber rocket starts to melt.

571
02:04:00,560 --> 02:04:12,160
Basically, carbon fiber and aluminum have about the same operating temperature capabilities, whereas steel can operate at twice the temperature. These are very rough approximations.

572
02:04:12,160 --> 02:04:18,720
I won't build the rocket. What I mean is people will say, "Oh, he said this twice. It's actually 0.8." I'm like, shut up, assholes.

573
02:04:18,720 --> 02:04:28,080
That's what the main comment's going to be about. God damn it. The point is, in retrospect, we should have started with steel in the beginning. It was dumb not to do steel.

574
02:04:28,080 --> 02:04:40,480
Okay, but to play this back to you, what I'm hearing is that steel was a riskier, less proven path, other than the early US rockets. Versus carbon fiber was a worse but

575
02:04:40,480 --> 02:04:52,800
more proven out path. So you need to be the one to push for, "Hey, we're going to do this riskier path and just figure it out." So you're fighting a sort of conservatism in a sense.

576
02:04:52,800 --> 02:05:02,800
That's why I initially said that the issue is that we weren't making fast enough progress. We were having trouble making even a small barrel section of the carbon fiber

577
02:05:02,800 --> 02:05:14,320
that didn't have wrinkles in it. Because at that large scale, you have to have many plies, many layers of the carbon fiber. You've got to cure it and you've got to cure it

578
02:05:14,320 --> 02:05:26,000
in such a way that it doesn't have any wrinkles or defects. Carbon fiber is much less resilient than steel. It has much less toughness.

579
02:05:26,000 --> 02:05:39,360
Stainless steel will stretch and bend, the carbon fiber will tend to shatter. Toughness being the area under the stress strain curve.

580
02:05:39,360 --> 02:05:51,200
You're generally going to have to do better with steel, but stainless steel to be precise. One other Starship question. So I visited Starbase, I think it was two years ago,

581
02:05:51,200 --> 02:06:02,880
with Sam Teller, and that was awesome. It was very cool to see, in a whole bunch of ways. One thing I noticed was that people really took pride in the simplicity of things, where everyone

582
02:06:02,880 --> 02:06:16,320
wants to tell you how Starship is just a big soda can, and we're hiring welders, and if you can weld in any industrial project, you can weld here. But there's a lot of pride in the simplicity.

583
02:06:16,320 --> 02:06:23,200
Well, factually Starship is a very complicated rocket. So that's what I'm getting at. Are things simple or are they complex?

584
02:06:23,200 --> 02:06:36,800
I think maybe just what they're trying to say is that you don't have to have prior experience in the rocket industry to work on Starship. Somebody just needs to be smart and work hard

585
02:06:36,800 --> 02:06:47,920
and be trustworthy and they can work on a rocket. They don't need prior rocket experience. Starship is the most complicated machine ever made by humans, by a long shot.

586
02:06:47,920 --> 02:07:08,800
In what regards? Anything, really. I'd say there isn't a more complex machine. I'd say that pretty much any project I can think of would be easier than this. That's why nobody has ever made a fully

587
02:07:08,800 --> 02:07:26,480
reusable orbital rocket. It's a very hard problem. Many smart people have tried before, very smart people with immense resources, and they failed. And we haven't succeeded yet. Falcon is partially

588
02:07:26,480 --> 02:07:41,920
reusable, but the upper stage is not. Starship Version 3, I think this design can be fully reusable. That full reusability is what will enable

589
02:07:41,920 --> 02:07:55,280
us to become a multi-planet civilization. Any technical problem, even like a Hadron Collider or something like that, is an easier problem than this.

590
02:07:55,280 --> 02:08:04,960
We spent a lot of time on bottlenecks. Can you say what the current Starship bottlenecks are, even at a high level? Trying to make it not explode, generally.

591
02:08:04,960 --> 02:08:13,680
It really wants to explode. That old chestnut. All those combustible materials. We've had two boosters explode on the test stand.

592
02:08:13,680 --> 02:08:25,920
One obliterated the entire test facility. So it only takes that one mistake. The amount of energy contained in a Starship is insane.

593
02:08:25,920 --> 02:08:37,120
Is that why it's harder than Falcon? It's because it's just more energy? It's a lot of new technology. It's pushing the performance envelope. The

594
02:08:37,120 --> 02:08:48,640
Raptor 3 engine is a very, very advanced engine. It's by far the best rocket engine ever made. But it desperately wants to blow up. Just to put things into perspective here,

595
02:08:48,640 --> 02:09:02,480
on liftoff the rocket is generating over 100 gigawatts of power. That’s 20% of US electricity. It's actually insane. It's a great comparison. While not exploding. Sometimes.

596
02:09:02,480 --> 02:09:12,800
Sometimes, yes. So I was like, how does it not explode? There's thousands of ways that it could explode and only one way that it doesn't.

597
02:09:12,800 --> 02:09:30,880
So we want it not only to really not explode, but fly reliably on a daily basis, like once per hour. Obviously, if it blows up a lot, it's very difficult to maintain that launch cadence. Yes.

598
02:09:30,880 --> 02:09:44,560
What's the single biggest remaining problem for Starship? It's having the heat shield be reusable. No one's ever made a reusable orbital heat shield.

599
02:09:44,560 --> 02:10:01,200
So the heat shield's gotta make it through the ascent phase without shucking a bunch of tiles, and then it's gotta come back in and also not lose a bunch of tiles or overheat the main airframe.

600
02:10:01,200 --> 02:10:09,440
Isn't that hard because it's fundamentally a consumable? Well, yes, but your brake pads in your car are also consumable, but they last a very long time.

601
02:10:09,440 --> 02:10:27,680
Fair. So it just needs to last a very long time. We have brought the ship back and had it do a soft landing in the ocean. We've done that a few times. But it lost a lot of tiles.

602
02:10:27,680 --> 02:10:40,080
It was not reusable without a lot of work. Even though it did come to a soft landing, it would not have been reusable without a lot of work.

603
02:10:40,080 --> 02:10:50,960
So it's not really reusable in that sense. That's the biggest problem that remains, a fully reusable heat shield. You want to be able to land it,

604
02:10:50,960 --> 02:11:06,720
refill propellant and fly again. You can't do this laborious inspection of 40,000 tiles type of thing. When I read biographies of yours,

605
02:11:06,720 --> 02:11:15,120
it seems like you're just able to drive the sense of urgency and drive the sense of "this is the thing that can scale." I'm curious why you

606
02:11:15,120 --> 02:11:24,480
think other organizations of your… SpaceX and Tesla are really big companies now. You're still able to keep that culture. What goes wrong with other companies such

607
02:11:24,480 --> 02:11:33,440
that they're not able to do that? I don't know. Like today, you said you had a bunch of SpaceX meetings. What is it that you're doing there that's keeping that?

608
02:11:33,440 --> 02:11:47,520
It’s adding urgency? Well, I don't know. I guess the urgency is going to come from whoever is leading the company. I have a maniacal sense of urgency.

609
02:11:47,520 --> 02:11:57,680
So that maniacal sense of urgency projects through the rest of the company. Is it because of consequences? They're like, "Elon set a crazy deadline, but if I don't get it,

610
02:11:57,680 --> 02:12:07,120
I know what happens to me." Is it just that you're able to identify bottlenecks and get rid of them so people can move fast? How do you think about why your companies are able to move fast?

611
02:12:07,120 --> 02:12:25,520
I'm constantly addressing the limiting factor. On the deadlines front, I generally actually try to aim for a deadline that I at least think is at the 50th percentile.

612
02:12:25,520 --> 02:12:42,400
So it's not like an impossible deadline, but it's the most aggressive deadline I can think of that could be achieved with 50% probability. Which means that it'll be late half the time.

613
02:12:42,400 --> 02:12:55,040
There is a law of gas expansion that applies to schedules. If you said we're going to do something in five years, which to me is like infinity time,

614
02:12:55,040 --> 02:13:07,840
it will expand to fill the available schedule and it'll take five years. Physics will limit how fast you can do certain things.

615
02:13:07,840 --> 02:13:17,520
So scaling up manufacturing, there's a rate at which you can move the atoms and scale manufacturing. That's why you can't instantly

616
02:13:17,520 --> 02:13:31,840
make a million units a year of something. You've got to design the manufacturing line. You've got to bring it up. You've got to ride the S-curve of production.

617
02:13:31,840 --> 02:13:54,640
What can I say that's actually helpful to people? Generally, a maniacal sense of urgency is a very big deal. You want to have an aggressive schedule and you want to figure out what the limiting

618
02:13:54,640 --> 02:14:07,840
factor is at any point in time and help the team address that limiting factor. So Starlink was slowly in the works for many years. We talked about it all the way in the beginning of the company.

619
02:14:07,840 --> 02:14:25,760
So then there was a team you had built in Redmond, and then at one point you decided this team is just not cutting it. It went for a few years slowly, and so why didn't

620
02:14:25,760 --> 02:14:38,880
you act earlier, and why did you act when you did? Why was that the right moment at which to act? I have these very detailed engineering reviews weekly.

621
02:14:38,880 --> 02:14:50,000
That's maybe a very unusual level of granularity. I don't know anyone who runs a company, or at least a manufacturing company, that goes with the level of detail that I go

622
02:14:50,000 --> 02:15:06,960
into. It's not as though... I have a pretty good understanding of what's actually going on because we go through things in detail. I'm a big believer in skip-level meetings

623
02:15:06,960 --> 02:15:25,440
where instead of having the person that reports to me say things, it's everyone that reports to them saying something in the technical review. And there can't be advanced preparation.

624
02:15:25,440 --> 02:15:37,520
Otherwise you're going to get "glazed", as I say these days. Exactly. Very Gen Z of you. How do you prevent advanced preparation? Do you call on them randomly? No, I just go around the room.

625
02:15:37,520 --> 02:15:56,240
Everyone provides an update. It's a lot of information to keep in your head. If you have meetings weekly or twice weekly, you've got a snapshot of what that person said.

626
02:15:56,240 --> 02:16:12,880
You can then plot the progress points. You can sort of mentally plot the points on a curve and say, "are we converging to a solution or not?"

627
02:16:12,880 --> 02:16:29,840
I'll take drastic action only when I conclude that success is not in a set of possible outcomes. So when I finally reach the conclusion that unless drastic action is done, we have no chance of

628
02:16:29,840 --> 02:16:45,439
success, then I must take drastic action. I came to that conclusion in 2018, took drastic action and fixed the problem. You've got many, many companies. In each of

629
02:16:45,439 --> 02:16:56,318
them it sounds like you do this kind of deep engineering understanding of what the relevant bottlenecks are so you can do these reviews with people.

630
02:16:56,318 --> 02:17:04,559
You've been able to scale it up to five, six, seven companies. Within one of these companies, you have many different mini companies within them.

631
02:17:04,559 --> 02:17:13,359
What determines the max amount here? Because you have like 80 companies…? 80? No. But you have so many already. That's already remarkable. By this current number.

632
02:17:13,359 --> 02:17:32,639
Exactly. We can barely keep one company together. It depends on the situation. I actually don't have regular meetings with The Boring Company,

633
02:17:32,639 --> 02:17:42,559
so The Boring Company is sort of cruising along. Basically, if something is working well and making good progress, then there's no point in me spending time on it.

634
02:17:42,559 --> 02:17:59,920
I actually allocate time according to where the limiting factor. Where are things problematic? Where are we pushing against? What is holding us back? I focus, at the risk of saying the

635
02:17:59,920 --> 02:18:12,799
words too many times, on the limiting factor. The irony is if something's going really well, they don't see much of me. But if something is going badly,

636
02:18:12,799 --> 02:18:21,120
they'll see a lot of me. Or not even badly… If something is the limiting factor. The limiting factor, exactly. It’s not exactly going badly but it’s the

637
02:18:21,120 --> 02:18:32,000
thing that we need to make go faster. When something’s a limiting factor at SpaceX or Tesla, are you talking weekly and daily with the engineer that's

638
02:18:32,000 --> 02:18:46,799
working on it? How does that actually work? Most things that are the limiting factor are weekly and some things are twice weekly. The AI5 chip review is twice weekly.

639
02:18:46,799 --> 02:19:03,760
Every Tuesday and Saturday is the chip review. Is it open ended in how long it goes? Technically, yes, but usually it's two or three hours. Sometimes less. It depends on

640
02:19:03,760 --> 02:19:11,840
how much information we've got to go through. That's another thing. I'm just trying to tease out the differences here because the outcomes seem quite different.

641
02:19:11,840 --> 02:19:20,000
I think it's interesting to know what inputs are different. It feels like in the corporate world, one, like you were saying, the CEO doing engineering

642
02:19:20,000 --> 02:19:32,318
reviews does not always happen despite the fact that that is what the company is doing. But then time is often pretty finely sliced into half hour meetings or even 15 minute meetings.

643
02:19:32,318 --> 02:19:43,040
It seems like you hold more open-ended, "We're talking about it until we figure it out" type things. Sometimes. But most

644
02:19:43,040 --> 02:20:04,479
of them seem to more or less stay on time. Today's Starship engineering review went a bit longer because there were more topics to discuss. They're trying to figure out how to scale to a

645
02:20:04,479 --> 02:20:15,080
million plus tons to orbit per year. It’s quite challenging. Can I ask a question? You said about Optimus and AI that they're going to result in double

646
02:20:15,080 --> 02:20:28,080
digit growth rates within a matter of years. Oh, like the economy? Yes. I think that's right. What was the point of the DOGE cuts if the economy is going to grow so much?

647
02:20:28,080 --> 02:20:41,600
Well, I think waste and fraud are not good things to have. I was actually pretty worried about... In the absence of AI and robotics,

648
02:20:41,600 --> 02:20:54,240
we're actually totally screwed because the national debt is piling up like crazy. The interest payments to national debt exceed the military budget, which is a trillion dollars.

649
02:20:54,240 --> 02:21:03,680
So we have over a trillion dollars just in interest payments. I was pretty concerned about that. Maybe if I spend some time, we can

650
02:21:03,680 --> 02:21:16,080
slow down the bankruptcy of the United States and give us enough time for the AI and robots to help solve the national debt. Or not help solve, it's the only

651
02:21:16,080 --> 02:21:30,880
thing that could solve the national debt. We are 1000% going to go bankrupt as a country, and fail as a country, without AI and robots. Nothing else will solve the national debt.

652
02:21:30,880 --> 02:21:43,920
We just need enough time to build the AI and robots to not go bankrupt before then. I guess the thing I'm curious about is, when DOGE starts you have this enormous

653
02:21:43,920 --> 02:21:53,920
ability to enact reform. Not that enormous. Sure. I totally buy your point that it's important that AI and robotics drive

654
02:21:53,920 --> 02:22:03,840
productivity improvements, drive GDP growth. But why not just directly go after the things you were pointing out, like the tariffs on certain components, or permitting?

655
02:22:03,840 --> 02:22:21,359
I'm not the president. And it is very hard to cut things that are obvious waste and fraud, like ridiculous waste and fraud. What I discovered is that it's extremely

656
02:22:21,359 --> 02:22:34,160
difficult even to cut very obvious waste and fraud from the government because the government has to operate on who's complaining. If you cut off payments to fraudsters,

657
02:22:34,160 --> 02:22:46,240
they immediately come up with the most sympathetic sounding reasons to continue the payment. They don't say, "Please keep the fraud going." They’re like, "You're killing baby pandas."

658
02:22:46,240 --> 02:22:56,399
Meanwhile, no baby pandas are dying. They're just making it up. The fraudsters are capable of coming up with extremely compelling, heart-wrenching stories that are false,

659
02:22:56,399 --> 02:23:16,240
but nonetheless sound sympathetic. That's what happened. Perhaps I should have known better. But I thought, wait, let's try to cut some amount of waste and pork from the government.

660
02:23:16,240 --> 02:23:30,880
Maybe there shouldn't be 20 million people marked as alive in Social Security who are definitely dead, and over the age of 115. The oldest American is 114. So it's safe to say if

661
02:23:30,880 --> 02:23:47,439
somebody is 115 and marked as alive in the Social Security database, there's either a typo… Somebody should call them and say, "We seem to have your birthday wrong, or we need to mark you

662
02:23:47,439 --> 02:23:59,920
as dead." One of the two things. Very intimidating call to get. Well, it seems like a reasonable thing. Say if their birthday is in the future

663
02:23:59,920 --> 02:24:13,760
and they have a Small Business Administration loan, and their birthday is 2165, we either have a typo or we have fraud. So we say, "we appear to have gotten the

664
02:24:13,760 --> 02:24:23,520
century of your birth incorrect." Or a great plot for a movie. Yes. That's what I mean by, ludicrous fraud. Were those people getting payments?

665
02:24:23,520 --> 02:24:37,920
Some were getting payments from Social Security. But the main fraud vector was to mark somebody as alive in Social Security and then use every other government payment system to basically do fraud.

666
02:24:37,920 --> 02:24:46,319
Because what those other government payment systems do, they would simply do an "are you alive" check to the Social Security database. It's a bank shot.

667
02:24:46,319 --> 02:24:55,359
What would you estimate is the total amount of fraud from this mechanism? By the way, the Government Accountability Office has done these estimates before. I'm

668
02:24:55,359 --> 02:25:08,000
not the only one. In fact, I think the GAO did an analysis, a rough estimate of fraud during the Biden administration, and calculated it at roughly half a trillion dollars.

669
02:25:08,000 --> 02:25:16,319
So don't take my word for it. Take a report issued during the Biden administration. How about that? From this Social Security mechanism?

670
02:25:16,319 --> 02:25:30,240
It's one of many. It's important to appreciate that the government is very ineffective at stopping fraud. It's not like a company where, with

671
02:25:30,240 --> 02:25:44,880
stopping fraud, you've got a motivation because it's affecting the earnings of your company. The government just prints more money. You need caring and competence. These are

672
02:25:44,880 --> 02:25:57,760
in short supply at the federal level. When you go to the DMV, do you think, "Wow, this is a bastion of competence"? Well, now imagine it's worse than the DMV

673
02:25:57,760 --> 02:26:08,640
because it's the DMV that can print money. At least the state level DMVs need to... The states more or less need to stay within their budget or they go bankrupt.

674
02:26:08,640 --> 02:26:28,560
But the federal government just prints more money. If there's actually half a trillion of fraud, why was it not possible to cut all that? You really have to stand back and recalibrate

675
02:26:28,560 --> 02:26:49,920
your expectations for competence. Because you're operating in a world where you've got to make ends meet. You've got to pay your bills... Find the microphones. Exactly. It's not like there's a giant,

676
02:26:49,920 --> 02:27:03,520
largely uncaring monster bureaucracy. It's a bunch of anachronistic computers that are just sending payments. One of the things that the DOGE

677
02:27:03,520 --> 02:27:19,200
team did sounds so simple and probably will save $100-200 billion a year. It was simply requiring payments from the main Treasury computer—which is called PAM,

678
02:27:19,200 --> 02:27:32,240
Payment Accounts Master or something like that, there's $5 trillion payments a year—that go out have a payment appropriation code. Make it mandatory, not optional, that you

679
02:27:32,240 --> 02:27:48,640
have anything at all in the comment field. You have to recalibrate how dumb things are. Payments were being sent out with no appropriation code, not checking back to any congressional

680
02:27:48,640 --> 02:27:59,120
appropriation, and with no explanation. This is why the Department of War, formerly the Department of Defense, cannot pass an audit, because the information is literally

681
02:27:59,120 --> 02:28:10,160
not there. Recalibrate your expectations. I want to better understand this half a trillion number, because there's an IG report in 2024. Why is it so low?

682
02:28:10,160 --> 02:28:17,200
Maybe, but we found that over seven years, the Social Security fraud they estimated was like $70 billion over seven years, so like $10 billion a year.

683
02:28:17,200 --> 02:28:26,399
So I'd be curious to see what the other $490 billion is. Federal government expenditures are $7.5 trillion a year.

684
02:28:26,399 --> 02:28:36,399
How competent do you think the government is? The discretionary spending there is like… 15%? But it doesn't matter. Most of the fraud is non-discretionary.

685
02:28:36,399 --> 02:28:52,399
It's basically fraudulent Medicare, Medicaid, Social Security, disability. There's a zillion government payments. A bunch of these payments are in

686
02:28:52,399 --> 02:29:04,479
fact block transfers to the states. So the federal government doesn't even have the information in a lot of cases to even know if there's fraud.

687
02:29:04,479 --> 02:29:18,479
Let's consider reductio ad absurdum. The government is perfect and has no fraud. What is your probability estimate of that? Zero. Okay, so then would you say, fraud and waste

688
02:29:18,479 --> 02:29:32,399
at the government is 90% efficient? That also would be quite generous. But if it's only 90%, that means that there's $750 billion a year of waste and

689
02:29:32,399 --> 02:29:43,280
fraud. And it's not 90%. It's not 90% effective. This seems like a strange way to first principles the amount of fraud in the government. Just like, how much do you think there is?

690
02:29:43,280 --> 02:29:49,680
Anyways, we don't have to do it live, but I'd be curious— You know a lot about fraud at Stripe? People are constantly trying to do fraud.

691
02:29:49,680 --> 02:29:58,720
Yeah, but as you say, it's a little bit of a... We've really ground it down, but it's a little bit of a different problem space because you're dealing with a much more heterogeneous set of

692
02:29:58,720 --> 02:30:07,840
fraud vectors here than we are. But at Stripe, you have high competence and you try hard. You have high competence and

693
02:30:07,840 --> 02:30:22,640
high caring, but still fraud is non-zero. Now imagine it's at a much bigger scale, there's much less competence, and much less caring. At PayPal back in the day, we tried to manage

694
02:30:22,640 --> 02:30:36,319
fraud down to about 1% of the payment volume. That was very difficult. It took a tremendous amount of competence and caring to get fraud merely to 1%. Now imagine that you're an organization where

695
02:30:36,319 --> 02:30:48,479
there's much less caring and much less competence. It's going to be much more than 1%. How do you feel now looking back on politics and doing stuff there?

696
02:30:48,479 --> 02:31:05,840
Looking from the outside in, two things have been quite impactful: one, the America PAC, and two, the acquisition of Twitter at the time. But also it seems like there

697
02:31:05,840 --> 02:31:27,200
was a bunch of heartache. What's your grading of the whole experience? I think those things needed to be done to maximize the probability that the future is good.

698
02:31:27,200 --> 02:31:41,520
Politics generally is very tribal. People lose their objectivity usually with politics. They generally have trouble seeing the good on the other side or the bad on their own side.

699
02:31:41,520 --> 02:31:52,080
That's generally how it goes. That, I guess, was one of the things that surprised me the most. You often simply cannot reason with people. If they're in one tribe or the other.

700
02:31:52,080 --> 02:32:07,760
They simply believe that everything their tribe does is good and anything the other political tribe does is bad. Persuading them otherwise is almost impossible.

701
02:32:07,760 --> 02:32:30,640
But I think overall those actions—acquiring Twitter, getting Trump elected, even though it makes a lot of people angry—I think those actions were good for civilization.

702
02:32:30,640 --> 02:32:42,000
How does it feed into the future you're excited about? Well, America needs to be strong enough to last long enough to extend life to other

703
02:32:42,000 --> 02:32:59,280
planets and to get AI and robotics to the point where we can ensure that the future is good. On the other hand, if we were to descend into, say, communism or some situation where the state

